 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-13",
  "asof": "2026-10-14 09:55 UTC",
  "headline": "The banks split and the tape froze \u2014 and the long end eased a third time before the print that settles it",
  "standfirst": "Nothing on the board moved more than six tenths of a sigma, and the S&P closed 1.79 points lower at 7,805.45 after moving 4.30 on Monday. The banks reported and split: JPMorgan missed at $5.57 against $5.93 expected, its first miss in nearly three years, while Goldman earned $17.01 against $12.25 a year ago on stock trading \u2014 and the Treasury fined Bank of America $1.2bn, its third-largest penalty ever, the same morning. With Treasuries trading again the ten-year fell about 5 basis points to roughly 5.19% and the thirty-year to around 5.56%, a third consecutive session against the term-premium story. In the book GOOG made a fourth closing high in five sessions and AAPL touched a 52-week high of 348.15 before closing 341.27, on the day Nikkei Asia reported iPhone 18 Pro demand has been weak since launch, while OKTA traded a 4.8% range to finish 0.24% lower and AMZN fell for a third session \u2014 a net of -2.55 on the day. Heat 3.05R against 3.0R, unchanged for a twenty-fourth session. September CPI lands Wednesday at 8:30 in New York.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "CPI Wednesday morning \u00b7 the foldable Friday",
    "title": "The print lands Wednesday at 8:30 New York time",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "CPI",
      "html": "<b>September CPI, 8:30 ET</b> \u2014 the only first-tier print before the Fed, and the arbiter of two ISM prices indices against a soft PCE",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-14",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo pre-orders open</b>, 5am Pacific \u2014 the first real read on whether buyers are waiting for the foldable rather than skipping the cycle",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-16",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo on sale</b> in the US and 70-plus countries, from <b>$1,999</b>",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision</b> and <b>Alphabet Q3</b> on the same day \u2014 the minutes promise another hike; the market prices roughly a 78% hold",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "AAPL \u00b7 AMZN",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4 and Amazon Q3 earnings</b> \u2014 the first iPhone 18 quarter against a demand report, and Amazon's first read on holiday demand",
      "when": "Thu \u00b7 2026-10-29",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>September CPI has to settle three arguments that have run all fortnight.</b> Whether the ISM prices indices at <b>77.9</b> and <b>74.0</b> are feeding through or stalling at the factory gate, when core PCE came in at <b>3.0% against 3.3% expected</b>. Whether the Fed's minutes and Waller's comments describe a committee that will hike again or one that is talking, with the market at roughly a <b>78% hold</b> for 28 October. And whether the long end is inflation or supply \u2014 where the evidence against the supply story is now <b>three consecutive sessions</b>, none of them driven by a release. Watch the energy line: gasoline was <b>up 27.4% year-on-year</b> in the last published report and crude has not come down since."
   }
  ],
  "ondeck": "<b>September CPI has to settle three arguments that have run all fortnight.</b> Whether the ISM prices indices at <b>77.9</b> and <b>74.0</b> are feeding through or stalling at the factory gate, when core PCE came in at <b>3.0% against 3.3% expected</b>. Whether the Fed's minutes and Waller's comments describe a committee that will hike again or one that is talking, with the market at roughly a <b>78% hold</b> for 28 October. And whether the long end is inflation or supply \u2014 where the evidence against the supply story is now <b>three consecutive sessions</b>, none of them driven by a release. Watch the energy line: gasoline was <b>up 27.4% year-on-year</b> in the last published report and crude has not come down since.",
